WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.600
What’s that bright thing in the sky?
Is it a&nbsp;blazing chariot?

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.240
An all-seeing eye? Maybe a burning ball of gas?

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:11.440
At different points in history, humans&nbsp;have&nbsp;
believed the sun to be all of these things.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.698
They may not have agreed on what the sun&nbsp;was,

00:00:13.698 --> 00:00:16.720
but ancient cultures recognized&nbsp;
its power to sustain life,

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:20.320
so it was appropriately held in a&nbsp;
place of honor in most mythologies.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.280
Whether people thought it was a divine being&nbsp;
or a nuclear fusion factory, which we now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:30.560
know to be the case, the stories all had one&nbsp;
lesson in common: don’t look directly at it.

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:38.960
Energy from the sun gives heat, offers a&nbsp;
bright reprieve from the darkness of night,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:41.680
and serves as the base
for our entire food chain.

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:44.960
Basically, the sun is central to so many myths

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:49.440
because&nbsp;it provides all the energy we&nbsp;
need to live. But it makes you wonder.

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.840
Where does all that energy even come from?

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:56.720
Well, let’s have fellow astronomer Matt&nbsp;
O’Dowd from PBS Digital’s Space Time

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:59.520
explain&nbsp;a little about the
source of the sun’s power.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:03.840
The Sun is a gigantic nuclear&nbsp;
reactor, as is every star.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:08.480
And I do mean gigantic. It's the same as&nbsp;
every nuclear bomb on the planet went off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:15.760
roughly 20 million times every second. It's&nbsp;
been doing that for 5 billion years so far,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:20.240
with another 5 billion to go. The secret&nbsp;
to this incredible energy production

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:22.800
comes from the most famous
equation of all time:

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.858
Einstein's E =&nbsp;mc².

00:01:25.858 --> 00:01:28.800
This tells us that mass is just
another form of energy

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:32.480
and that every little bit of mass&nbsp;
contains an enormous amount of energy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:36.160
And that's the type of energy&nbsp;
released in the center of the Sun.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:42.720
The tiny nuclei of the tiniest atom - hydrogen&nbsp;
- are smashed together at incredible speeds,

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.920
allowing them to overcome
their natural repulsion

00:01:45.920 --> 00:01:48.240
and&nbsp;stick together due
to the strong nuclear force.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:55.360
The result is a nucleus of helium plus a LOT&nbsp;
of energy. This is a bit of stellar alchemy:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:59.920
the elements of the periodic table&nbsp;
are forged in the&nbsp;hearts of stars,

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:04.400
although most of the interesting ones get&nbsp;
made by stars much bigger than our sun,

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:07.840
before being blasted out in supernova explosions.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:13.040
The energy produced in these&nbsp;
fusion reactions takes the form of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:19.840
gamma ray light. This light slowly makes its&nbsp;
way out of the super-dense interior of the Sun.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:25.280
It takes 10s of thousands of&nbsp;
years for each photon of light

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:30.160
to&nbsp;bounce its way out. And then takes&nbsp;
about eight minutes to reach the earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:34.800
once it escapes. So the warmth&nbsp;you&nbsp;
feel on your face when you step out

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.240
into a&nbsp;&nbsp;summer day was actually&nbsp;
produced in the core of&nbsp;the Sun

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.600
before the dawn of human civilization.

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:44.880
And since that dawn, cultures&nbsp;
have been worshiping&nbsp;the sun

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:47.120
and its light as a powerful deity.

00:02:47.120 --> 00:02:51.040
Think about it. The world seems to&nbsp;
come alive when the sun is in the sky,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:55.200
and its reliable return every day&nbsp;
provides a sense of order to the universe.

00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:00.000
Of course, when ancient Indian cultures&nbsp;
in the Vedic period about 3000 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:05.040
ago looked at the Sun, they knew nothing&nbsp;
about the strong nuclear force. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:09.200
they saw the highest representation&nbsp;
of Agni, the Vedic god of fire.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:12.800
Often depicted with two heads and riding a ram,&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:18.320
Agni was one of the most powerful Vedic gods,&nbsp;
second only to Indra, the god of the heavens.

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:23.600
In the other levels of his power, Agni&nbsp;
existed on Earth as regular fire and in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:28.480
the sky as lightning. In his triumvirate&nbsp;
form, he came to represent the&nbsp;connection

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:30.960
between the mundane and the divine.

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:36.320
Just like the fire and sun he personified,&nbsp;
Agni was feared as an agent of destruction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:39.600
but also revered for his ability to nourish.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:43.680
But Agni wasn’t always pleased by&nbsp;
his association with destruction.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:46.800
In one story from the Vedas, a demon asked Agni&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:51.840
to confirm his rightful betrothal&nbsp;
to Puloma, wife of the sage Bhrigu.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:57.120
It was a complicated situation, and Agni could&nbsp;
neither confirm nor deny the demon’s claim,

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:59.520
so the demon stole Puloma away.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:04.160
She made it back okay, but Bhrigu&nbsp;
still cursed Agni to devour and destroy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.160 --> 00:04:10.560
everything in his path. Enraged, Agni protested&nbsp;
by withdrawing all of his fire from the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.840
including the fire that humans&nbsp;
used to sacrifice to the gods.

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:19.040
Obviously, this would not do, so Brahma,&nbsp;
the supreme creator of the universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:23.520
altered the curse so that Agni’s fire&nbsp;
would purify everything he touched.&nbsp;

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:28.880
Agni isn’t the oldest solar deity — it’s likely&nbsp;
impossible to know who that first one was.

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:33.520
The Vedic culture was just one of many&nbsp;
who devoted a lot of time, energy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:35.440
and resources to worshiping the sun.

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:40.160
The Incas who lived in modern day&nbsp;
Peru from the 12th to 16th centuries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:43.360
incorporated the Sun in so&nbsp;
many aspects of their&nbsp;culture,

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:46.160
from holidays to agriculture to politics.

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:50.240
Their sun god, Inti, was one of&nbsp;
their most powerful deities, again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:53.520
second only to his father,&nbsp;
the creator god Viracocha.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:57.280
The Incan royal family was said to&nbsp;
be directly descended from Inti,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:59.440
so his temples were the most grand.

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:02.000
The Temple of the Sun in the capital city Cuzco&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:06.800
was made of ornately carved stone and&nbsp;
filled with extravagant gold objects,

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:11.440
all of which was plundered when the Spanish&nbsp;
conquered the Inca Empire in the 1500s.

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:15.840
Inti was honored every year with a&nbsp;
week-long festival called Inti Raymi.

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:19.840
The festival includes fasting,&nbsp;
sacrificing scores of animals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:24.160
parading decorated statues of Inti,&nbsp;
and of course singing and dancing.

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:29.040
It’s a party, after all, but it was&nbsp;
also meant as an appeal to Inti.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:31.200
The festival,&nbsp;&nbsp;which is still held today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:34.880
happens In June, around the southern&nbsp;
hemisphere’s winter solstice.

00:05:34.880 --> 00:05:39.680
In their celebration they were begging Inti&nbsp;
to return to them in the darkest time of year

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:43.440
Of course, not all solar&nbsp;
deities were depicted as male.

00:05:43.440 --> 00:05:46.880
In Japanese shinto religion,&nbsp;
Amaterasu is&nbsp;considered

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:49.920
the most powerful kami, or divine&nbsp;&nbsp;spirit.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:55.200
She is the ruler of the Takama no Hara, the&nbsp;
high celestial plain where all the kami live.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.920 --> 00:06:00.720
Born from the left eye of her father&nbsp;
Izanagi as he purified himself in a stream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.720 --> 00:06:05.040
Amaterasu is also said to be the&nbsp;
ancestor of the&nbsp;Japanese ruling family.

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:10.480
In one myth, Amaterasu&nbsp;hides in a&nbsp;
cave after the storm god Susanoo

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:13.920
pulls&nbsp;some distasteful&nbsp;
younger brother shenanigans.

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:16.480
Without Amaterasu’s warming light, the world

00:06:16.480 --> 00:06:20.000
fell&nbsp;into decay and evil spirits roamed the Earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.000 --> 00:06:24.480
The other kami tried everything they could&nbsp;
think of to draw Amaterasu out of the cave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.480 --> 00:06:29.600
from using roosters to trick her into thinking&nbsp;
it was dawn to bribing her with sparkly jewels.

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:33.760
She was eventually lured out&nbsp;
of hiding by the kamis’ raucous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:38.480
laughter as they watched the goddess&nbsp;
Amenouzume perform a naughty dance.

00:06:38.480 --> 00:06:44.480
In Baltic mythology, the sun was represented by&nbsp;
Saule, the mother and ruler of the heavenly beings

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:46.800
who&nbsp;determined the well-being&nbsp;
of all life on earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.360 --> 00:06:50.960
Depicted as a blond woman&nbsp;
wearing a golden shawl and crown,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:55.680
Saule lived in a palace at the end of the sea,&nbsp;
where she tended a garden of golden apples.

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:02.080
According to legend, Saule in her divine radiance
would ride across the sky in a chariot pulled by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:02.080 --> 00:07:07.040
two tireless white horses who didn’t&nbsp;
sweat. Her daughters were the planets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:12.560
and her husband was the moon. Though in&nbsp;
some&nbsp;stories, Saule and the moon are divorced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:18.640
because he decided to pursue the goddess of&nbsp;
dawn, aka the morning star or the planet Venus.

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:24.800
Saule was far from the only solar deity to ride&nbsp;
a&nbsp;vehicle across the sky. The Greek gods Helios

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:28.400
and Apollo were both shown riding&nbsp;
a chariot through&nbsp;the heavens.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:32.160
The Egyptian sun god Ra was&nbsp;
said&nbsp;to ride in a solar barge

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:37.360
during the day. At night,&nbsp;he sailed through the&nbsp;
underworld until he returned to the sky in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:42.240
east. Cave carvings from the neolithic&nbsp;
and bronze ages depicting the Sun on

00:07:42.240 --> 00:07:47.600
a ship passing through stars show that the concept
of a solar barge has existed for millennia.

00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:50.640
Given how important the sun&nbsp;
was to these ancient&nbsp;cultures,

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:54.400
it’s no surprise that a solar&nbsp;
eclipse, a phenomenon where the moon

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:56.880
blocks the light of&nbsp;the Sun from our earthly view,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:01.680
would be a dramatic and meaningful&nbsp;
event. As Ra passed through the&nbsp;Egyptian

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:06.000
underworld in his boat, the evil&nbsp;
god Apep would try to devour him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:08.080
Eclipses happened when&nbsp;he succeeded,

00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:10.480
but Ra always managed to escape.

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:14.560
Plenty of other cultures explained&nbsp;
eclipses as something eating the sun.

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:18.720
In Vietnamese mythology,&nbsp;that&nbsp;
creature is a frog or toad.

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:22.320
In ancient&nbsp;China, it was a&nbsp;
dragon. In Hindu mythology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:25.920
the god Rahu was beheaded,&nbsp;
and he swallowed the&nbsp;sun

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:30.240
as his severed head flew through the&nbsp;
air. In various parts of the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:35.440
including parts of China, South America,&nbsp;
and even at the Griffith Observatory in LA,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:39.440
it’s customary to make loud&nbsp;
noises like banging pots and pans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.440 --> 00:08:44.400
to scare away the animal or demon trying&nbsp;
to eat or steal the sun during an eclipse.

00:08:44.400 --> 00:08:50.000
But to the Fon people of modern day Benin, a&nbsp;
solar eclipse was cause to celebrate, not fear.

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:54.640
Also known as the Dahomey, the Fon&nbsp;
believed that a supreme being named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:58.960
Nana Buluku created the universe&nbsp;
and gave birth to Mawu and Lisa.

00:08:59.600 --> 00:09:04.720
Mawu created the Earth with the help of a&nbsp;
great snake and then invited her brother Lisa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:10.720
to help her populate it with their children:&nbsp;
gods, goddesses, plants, animals, and humans.

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:16.880
The humans treated poor Mawu so terribly that&nbsp;
she fled to the sky and inhabited the moon.

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:21.280
But, she took pity on them and&nbsp;
sent her brother/baby daddy Lisa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:26.480
to teach the humans how to build shelter,&nbsp;
use tools, and farm. When he was done,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:30.000
he went up to the sky and inhabited&nbsp;
the sun to be close to his sister/wife.

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:35.040
When Mawu first went to visit&nbsp;
Lisa, she blocked his sunlight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:39.600
but it was okay because the fruit&nbsp;
of that union… was literal fruit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:43.360
They were the seeds that humans needed&nbsp;
to plant once they mastered farming.

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:46.240
And now when there’s a solar eclipse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:51.040
the Fon who hold onto the old ways hope&nbsp;
there might be another gift from above.

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:55.520
From fiery gods of destruction to sweet&nbsp;
life givers who farm apples and protect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.520 --> 00:10:00.480
orphans, the sun has been a central figure&nbsp;
in some of the world’s most enduring myths.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:05.280
That’s because, as humans, there would be no&nbsp;
life without that nuclear force in the sky.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:09.680
I guess that makes it pretty important…&nbsp;
but in the grand scheme of things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:13.120
it’s still just a boring, middle-aged G-type star.

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:16.800
To learn just how not-special the sun is,

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:20.000
check&nbsp;out our collab episode with
PBS Space Time 

00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:21.887
about galactic habitable zones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.887 --> 00:10:25.360
featuring your other&nbsp;favorite&nbsp;
astrophysicist host: Matt O’Dowd.

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:29.600
I’ll&nbsp;also be there to answer the question:&nbsp;
if there are aliens in the Milky Way,

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:31.397
where should they be?

00:10:31.397 --> 00:10:34.194
While you’re there, be sure to check out the&nbsp;other episodes. 

00:10:34.194 --> 00:10:38.890
Space Time explores the outer&nbsp;
reaches of space, the craziness of astrophysics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.890 --> 00:10:42.887
and the possibilities of sci-fi, and anything&nbsp;
else you can think of beyond Planet Earth.

